<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ming Yuzhen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1331">1331</a>–1366</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ming Yuzhen was a commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-shouhui">Xu Shouhui</a>'s Red Turban regime who led an army up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> in 1357. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a> murdered Xu in 1360, he refused to accept Chen and in 1362 proclaimed himself emperor of Great Xia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, where his rule was remembered as mild. He died in 1366, and the state of his young son was conquered by the Ming in 1371.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ming Yuzhen came from a family of landowners in the village of Meiqiu in Sui County, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>. He served as an officer of the local militia, commanding patrolling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a> broke out the village council entrusted him with the defence of the town. Around 1352 he joined the rebels and rose quickly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> state, known to historians as Tianwan, serving under Ni Wenjun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sent to find food for the army in 1357, he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> fleet up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> from Yiling and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>, whose defence was weakened by a feud between the Yuan garrison commander Yang Han and the provincial governor Öljeitu. Xu Shouhui made him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, where he fought Yuan forces based at Jiading and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> until 1360 and turned back an invasion by the Green Turban Army of Li Xixi in 1358. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a> seized power, Ming warned Xu of him, and after learning of Xu's murder he recalled the troops he had sent to help Chen against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> and cut all ties with Chen.</p><p>In 1360 he declared himself King of Longshu, and in 1362, urged by his chief minister Liu Zhen, emperor of the Great Xia. He held Jiezhou in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> but failed against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanzhong">Hanzhong</a>, and in 1363 his minister Wan Sheng took Zhongqing, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a>, before the Yuan armies drove the Xia back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. He then built a government on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a> and, from 1363, on Yuan offices such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-secretariat">Central Secretariat</a>, calling himself the Lord of Light while supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, which won him the trust of the people and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died of illness in 1366 and was succeeded by his nine-year-old son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-sheng">Ming Sheng</a>. The ministers Wan Sheng and Dai Shou fought for control of the young emperor, and Wan was killed; in 1367 the official Wu Youren rose against Dai, and the two came to terms. Without firm leadership the Xia waited on events, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> in 1368, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> in 1371 without facing any major obstacles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ming Yuzhen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chen ruled by force rather than by building institutions, and the murder of Xu Shouhui led commanders such as <b>Ming Yuzhen</b> in Sichuan to break away.</blockquote><a href="/state/chen-han">Chen Han</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ming Yuzhen</b>, a commander of Xu Shouhui's Red Turban regime, led an army up the Yangtze into Sichuan in 1357 and took Chongqing.</blockquote><a href="/state/ming-xia">Ming Xia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suizhou">Suizhou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.72,113.37&amp;year=1353" aria-label="Suizhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ming Yuzhen"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4518 4839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4518 4839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.72,113.37&amp;year=1353">What was here in 1353? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
